          {
            "oid": "3cd1b72679675ca038ef9312efa895a5e863ef7e",
            "url": "/flazouh/githubpro/commit/3cd1b72679675ca038ef9312efa895a5e863ef7e",
            "authoredDate": "2026-07-31T18:20:23.000+02:00",
            "committedDate": "2026-07-31T18:20:23.000+02:00",
            "shortMessage": "Columns in the list, so a row is read down the page and not across it",
            "shortMessageMarkdown": null,
            "shortMessageMarkdownLink": "<a data-pjax=\"true\" title=\"Columns in the list, so a row is read down the page and not across it\n\nA row was a line of flexbox: every fact sized to its own contents, a spacer in\nthe middle, the halves pushed apart. Read one row at a time that is correct, and\nnobody reads a list one row at a time — so the checks landed wherever the title\nran out, the addresses started somewhere new on every line, and a column of facts\nthat all mean the same thing zig-zagged down the page.\n\nFixed tracks instead, measured off a real Working Set rather than guessed, with\nevery cell drawn on every row whether or not it holds anything: a row that skips\nits empty cells slides the ones after it into the wrong track, which is the ragged\nedge this is here to fix. The title takes what is left, because it is the part\nworth the width.\n\nTwo of the columns are only reserved when the list has something to put in them.\nA Working Set where no row has a reason should not hold seven rems open on every\nline for the reason none of them has, and the decision is taken from every row\nrather than the filtered ones, so typing in the filter box does not re-cut the\ncolumns under the reader&#39;s eyes.\n\nWhere it stands is now one column rather than two: GitHub&#39;s reason if they gave\none, the review decision otherwise. Both answer the same question, and holding\nfourteen rems for a pair that rarely arrive together is width taken from titles.\n\nThe repository is its own name now, not the whole address — `octo-org/\nori` was twelve rems of monospace to say `octo-repo`, and the owner is in the picture\nbeside it. The address in full is on the row for a pointer that rests there and\nfor anything reading it aloud.\n\nCo-authored-by: Cursor &lt;cursoragent@cursor.com&gt;\" class=\"color-fg-default\" href=\"/flazouh/githubpro/commit/3cd1b72679675ca038ef9312efa895a5e863ef7e\">Columns in the list, so a row is read down the page and not across it</a>",
            "bodyMessageHtml": "A row was a line of flexbox: every fact sized to its own contents, a spacer in\nthe middle, the halves pushed apart. Read one row at a time that is correct, and\nnobody reads a list one row at a time — so the checks landed wherever the title\nran out, the addresses started somewhere new on every line, and a column of facts\nthat all mean the same thing zig-zagged down the page.\n\nFixed tracks instead, measured off a real Working Set rather than guessed, with\nevery cell drawn on every row whether or not it holds anything: a row that skips\nits empty cells slides the ones after it into the wrong track, which is the ragged\nedge this is here to fix. The title takes what is left, because it is the part\nworth the width.\n\nTwo of the columns are only reserved when the list has something to put in them.\nA Working Set where no row has a reason should not hold seven rems open on every\nline for the reason none of them has, and the decision is taken from every row\nrather than the filtered ones, so typing in the filter box does not re-cut the\ncolumns under the reader's eyes.\n\nWhere it stands is now one column rather than two: GitHub's reason if they gave\none, the review decision otherwise. Both answer the same question, and holding\nfourteen rems for a pair that rarely arrive together is width taken from titles.\n\nThe repository is its own name now, not the whole address — `octo-org/\nori` was twelve rems of monospace to say `octo-repo`, and the owner is in the picture\nbeside it. The address in full is on the row for a pointer that rests there and\nfor anything reading it aloud.\n\nCo-authored-by: Cursor &lt;cursoragent@cursor.com&gt;",
            "authors": [
              {
